Hi Mateusz,

I read through this thread, I also ran into similar situation.

my experimental design is that I have two conditions, each with duplicates, since it's immunopreicipation samples, negative controls are expected to have some completely missing proteins. There is one known interacting protein, which has two peptides/features quantified in both replicates of the bait IP condition but none in the control IP condition, therefore, each feature only got 2 measurements and got removed by the first step of dataProcess, removingfewmeasurement, and I didn't notice where to turn it off.

Some convention also filter out things observed in less than 70% of runs, but in IP settings, let's say I have more than just bait vs. negative control IP, but several differentially treated bait IP conditions, while only one of the conditions having measurements for a feature, in a duplicate setting, it would be only two measurements, and by the rule of this convention and also MSStats dataProcess function, the feature would be removed, from biological point of view, it wouldn't make sense.

Combining both of our cases, to keep features having measurements in over 50% of the runs from the same condition seems make the most sense, or impute the rest of runs in this case. Wondering if there's any option that I wasn't aware of to fix my problem, or should I also need to expect the such fix in the future update?

Best,
Weixian

